All scripts work on Windows PowerShell 5.1+ and are for reading and investigating only. Reading the Security log requires an elevated (administrator) session.

1. Failed logons (4625): brute force
powershell
Get-WinEvent -FilterHashtable @{LogName='Security'; Id=4625; StartTime=(Get-Date).AddHours(-24)} |
ForEach-Object {
    $x = [xml]$_.ToXml()
    [pscustomobject]@{
        Time      = $_.TimeCreated
        User      = ($x.Event.EventData.Data | ? Name -eq 'TargetUserName').'#text'
        SourceIP  = ($x.Event.EventData.Data | ? Name -eq 'IpAddress').'#text'
        LogonType = ($x.Event.EventData.Data | ? Name -eq 'LogonType').'#text'
    }
} | Group-Object SourceIP, User | Sort-Object Count -Descending | Select-Object -First 20 Count, Name

Many attempts from one IP across many users suggests password spraying. Many attempts against a single user suggests brute force.

2. Successful logons after failures (4624)
powershell
Get-WinEvent -FilterHashtable @{LogName='Security'; Id=4624; StartTime=(Get-Date).AddHours(-24)} |
ForEach-Object {
    $x = [xml]$_.ToXml()
    [pscustomobject]@{
        Time      = $_.TimeCreated
        User      = ($x.Event.EventData.Data | ? Name -eq 'TargetUserName').'#text'
        SourceIP  = ($x.Event.EventData.Data | ? Name -eq 'IpAddress').'#text'
        LogonType = ($x.Event.EventData.Data | ? Name -eq 'LogonType').'#text'
    }
} | Where-Object { $_.LogonType -in 3,10 -and $_.SourceIP -notin '-','::1','127.0.0.1' }

Logon type 10 is RDP; type 3 is network (SMB, etc.). A run of 4625s followed by a 4624 from the same IP means the guessing may have succeeded.

3. Account creation and changes (4720, 4722, 4724, 4728, 4732, 4756)
powershell
Get-WinEvent -FilterHashtable @{LogName='Security'; Id=4720,4722,4724,4728,4732,4756; StartTime=(Get-Date).AddDays(-7)} |
Select-Object TimeCreated, Id, @{n='Message';e={$_.Message.Split("`n")[0]}}

A new user, or someone added to Administrators outside a normal process, is a common sign of persistence.

4. Log clearing (1102, 104)
powershell
Get-WinEvent -FilterHashtable @{LogName='Security'; Id=1102} -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
Get-WinEvent -FilterHashtable @{LogName='System'; Id=104} -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue

Someone is covering their tracks. This is almost always an escalation.

5. New services (7045) and scheduled tasks
powershell
# New services
Get-WinEvent -FilterHashtable @{LogName='System'; Id=7045; StartTime=(Get-Date).AddDays(-7)} |
Select-Object TimeCreated, @{n='Info';e={$_.Message}}

# All non-Microsoft tasks
Get-ScheduledTask | Where-Object { $_.TaskPath -notlike '\Microsoft\*' } |
Select-Object TaskName, TaskPath, State, @{n='Action';e={($_.Actions | % { "$($_.Execute) $($_.Arguments)" }) -join '; '}}

Services and scheduled tasks are classic persistence mechanisms.

6. Process creation (4688) with command line
powershell
Get-WinEvent -FilterHashtable @{LogName='Security'; Id=4688; StartTime=(Get-Date).AddHours(-6)} |
ForEach-Object {
    $x = [xml]$_.ToXml()
    [pscustomobject]@{
        Time    = $_.TimeCreated
        Proc    = ($x.Event.EventData.Data | ? Name -eq 'NewProcessName').'#text'
        Parent  = ($x.Event.EventData.Data | ? Name -eq 'ParentProcessName').'#text'
        CmdLine = ($x.Event.EventData.Data | ? Name -eq 'CommandLine').'#text'
    }
} | Where-Object { $_.CmdLine -match 'powershell|cmd|wscript|mshta|rundll32|certutil|bitsadmin' }

The command line only appears in 4688 if process-creation auditing is enabled and the “Include command line in process creation events” policy is on. A winword.exe → powershell.exe chain is suspicious.

7. PowerShell Script Block Logging (4104)
powershell
Get-WinEvent -FilterHashtable @{LogName='Microsoft-Windows-PowerShell/Operational'; Id=4104; StartTime=(Get-Date).AddDays(-1)} |
Where-Object { $_.Message -match 'FromBase64String|-enc|IEX|Invoke-Expression|DownloadString|Net.WebClient|bypass' } |
Select-Object TimeCreated, Message

This shows encoded and obfuscated commands in their decoded form.

8. Network connections mapped to processes
powershell
Get-NetTCPConnection -State Established |
Select-Object LocalPort, RemoteAddress, RemotePort, OwningProcess,
  @{n='Process';e={(Get-Process -Id $_.OwningProcess -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue).Path}} |
Where-Object { $_.RemoteAddress -notmatch '^(127\.|10\.|192\.168\.|172\.(1[6-9]|2\d|3[01])\.)' } |
Sort-Object Process

Look for processes running from Temp, AppData, or Downloads that have external connections.

9. Autostart locations (registry and folders)
powershell
$keys = 'HKLM:\Software\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Run',
        'HKCU:\Software\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Run',
        'HKLM:\Software\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\RunOnce'
foreach ($k in $keys) { "== $k"; Get-ItemProperty $k | Select-Object * -ExcludeProperty PS* }
Get-ChildItem "$env:APPDATA\Microsoft\Windows\Start Menu\Programs\Startup"
10. File hash and signature check
powershell
$f = 'C:\path\to\file.exe'
Get-FileHash $f -Algorithm SHA256
Get-AuthenticodeSignature $f | Select-Object Status, SignerCertificate

Then look the hash up in VirusTotal. Don’t upload internal company files there without permission; search by hash only.

11. Local administrators and users
powershell
Get-LocalGroupMember -Group 'Administrators'
Get-LocalUser | Select-Object Name, Enabled, LastLogon, PasswordLastSet
12. Defender detections
powershell
Get-MpThreatDetection | Select-Object InitialDetectionTime, ProcessName, Resources, ThreatID
Get-MpThreat
Get-MpComputerStatus | Select-Object AMServiceEnabled, RealTimeProtectionEnabled, AntivirusSignatureLastUpdated

Disabled real-time protection is, by itself, a reason to raise an alert.
